{"title": "The Lantern of the River Speaker", "body": "In a valley where rain spoke in whispers and rivers remembered every color of the sky, there lived an old boatman named Liora. She tended the Lantern of the River Speaker, a brass lamp that hung above her boat like a captive moon. It could listen, and it listened well. When the river dreamed, the lantern woke and offered guidance to whoever asked with a calm, wind-kissed voice.

One dry season, the river ran thin and the fish forgot their names. The people grew restless, for the river fed their stories as much as their crops. A child named Niko climbed into Liora’s boat with a question heavier than the oars: What will we do when the river forgets us? The lantern’s flame trembled and sang a low note, a note that sounded like rain on clay pots. It spoke of listening first, then acting, of weaving gratitude into every gesture: each fish spared, each stone left unbroken, each word spoken softly to the earth.

Guided by the lamp, Liora and Niko sailed to the heart of the valley, where the river’s memory slept beneath a bed of reeds. They woke it with patient questions and a chorus of thanks. The river remembered—blooming back into song, the fish singing along, the fields drinking light again. The Lantern never shone for greed but for listening, for keeping memory alive as a trusted friend.""}

